{"task_id": "Mbpp/418", "solution": "def Find_Max(input_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the element in a list that has the maximum length.\n    Returns the first such element encountered if there are ties.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not input_list:\n        return None\n\n    max_element = input_list[0]\n    max_length = len(input_list[0])\n\n    for element in input_list[1:]:\n        current_length = len(element)\n        if current_length > max_length:\n            max_length = current_length\n            max_element = element\n            \n    return max_element"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\ndef max_Product(arr: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a pair with the highest product from a given array of integers.\n    The array is expected to have at least two integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    max1 = max2 = -float('inf')  # Largest and second largest\n    min1 = min2 = float('inf')   # Smallest and second smallest\n\n    for x in arr:\n        if x > max1:\n            max2, max1 = max1, x\n        elif x > max2:\n            max2 = x\n\n        if x < min1:\n            min2, min1 = min1, x\n        elif x < min2:\n            min2 = x\n\n    # Calculate products of the two largest and two smallest numbers\n    prod1 = max1 * max2\n    prod2 = min1 * min2\n\n    if prod1 > prod2:\n        return (min(max1, max2), max(max1, max2))\n    elif prod2 > prod1:\n        return (min(min1, min2), max(min1, min2))\n    else:\n        # Tie-breaking logic (BR3): prefer larger max element; if tie persists, prefer larger min element\n        cand1_sorted = (min(max1, max2), max(max1, max2))\n        cand2_sorted = (min(min1, min2), max(min1, min2))\n\n        if cand1_sorted[1] != cand2_sorted[1]:\n            return cand1_sorted if cand1_sorted[1] > cand2_sorted[1] else cand2_sorted\n        else:\n            return cand1_sorted if cand1_sorted[0] > cand2_sorted[0] else cand2_sorted\n\n```"}
